# Target C-II — failure of a linear TB1 population forces a growing exact-phase block with growing many wide force diamonds

**Date:** 2026-09-17
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed
**Scope:** unbounded canonical high-`t` survivor sequences through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`
**Dependencies:** linear one-sided rectangle shore and joint-cost budget; non-antipodal TB2 localization; C67 direction separation; exact-antipodal exact force pairs; force-carrying robust fork; phase-block force monotonicity
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_growing_exact_phase_force_block_t18687.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The fixed-threshold density theorems leave a choice between strong TB1 interfaces and exact-antipodal interfaces.  By allowing the local cost threshold to grow slowly, one can sharpen the second branch qualitatively: unless non-antipodal TB1 interfaces themselves occur at positive linear density, **almost every interface on a linear one-sided shore is exactly antipodal**.  The exact interfaces then form only `o(D)` runs, so one run must grow without bound.

The force-carrying robust theorem adds a second conclusion.  On that same branch, a fixed positive density of exact-antipodal interfaces carries fixed-angle exact force diamonds.  Since there are only `o(D)` exact-phase runs, one growing exact-phase run contains a growing number of those wide force diamonds.

### Theorem GF — growing exact-phase force block dichotomy

Let an unbounded canonical survivor sequence remain in the high-`t` branch through `t<=1.8687`.  After passage to a subsequence, at least one of the following holds.

#### GF-A: linear non-antipodal TB1 obstruction

There are constants `epsilon>0` and `rho>0`, independent of `D`, such that at least

\[
\epsilon D
\tag{GF.1}
\]

one-sided interfaces have a TB1 switching diameter bar lying at least `rho` from both local dual-cone rays.  Hence every such bar has a fixed local opening margin

\[
\boxed{
 n_i\cdot w\ge D\sin\rho
}
\tag{GF.2}

for every unit local opening direction `w`.

#### GF-B: growing exact-phase force block

There is a consecutive block of row interfaces

\[
I_D^{\rm block}=[p_D,q_D]
\tag{GF.3}
\]

whose length tends to infinity,

\[
\boxed{q_D-p_D+1\longrightarrow\infty,}
\tag{GF.4}
\]

such that every internal interface is exactly antipodal.  Equivalently all row vectors in the block are exactly equal:

\[
\boxed{
u_{p_D}=u_{p_D+1}=\cdots=u_{q_D+1}=:v_D.}
\tag{GF.5}

Moreover, with the fixed angle

\[
\boxed{
\rho_0={c_0\over12},
\qquad
c_0={113\over20{,}000{,}000},
}
\tag{GF.6}

this single exact-phase block contains a number `W_D` of `rho_0`-wide **exact force-carrying** antipodal interfaces satisfying

\[
\boxed{W_D\longrightarrow\infty.}
\tag{GF.7}

At every one of those `W_D` interfaces two switching actual diameter bars:

1. cross at projective angle at least `rho_0`;
2. positively generate the exact strip reaction;
3. form a four-endpoint diamond containing one common radius-`Theta(D)` disk about the source circumcenter;
4. have diagonal intersection in one `O(D^(3/4))` central ball.

Across the entire exact-phase block the cut-resultant magnitudes are strictly monotone and the axial reactions all point in the same ordinary direction:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{i=p_D}^{q_D}|G_i|
=|A_{p_D}-A_{q_D+1}|<D.
}
\tag{GF.8}

Thus the non-TB1 branch contains a growing ordered plateau with growing many fixed-angle, central, force-carrying diameter dipoles rather than a collection of isolated exact-antipodal defects.

---

## 1. Use a slowly growing local-complexity threshold

Retain the same consecutive one-sided interface shore `I_D` from the linear high-`t` rectangle.  It satisfies

\[
|I_D|>{c\over2}D-o(D),
\qquad
c={113\over5{,}000{,}000}.
\tag{GF.9}
\]

The joint costs obey

\[
\sum_iJ_i<CcD+o(D),
\qquad C=1{,}109{,}000.
\tag{GF.10}
\]

Set

\[
M_D=\sqrt D
\tag{GF.11}
\]

and call an interface **slow-good** when

\[
J_i<CM_D=C\sqrt D.
\tag{GF.12}
\]

If `B_D` is the slow-bad set, then

\[
C\sqrt D\,|B_D|
\le CcD+o(D),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{|B_D|=o(D).}
\tag{GF.13}

Indeed the displayed bound is `O(sqrt D)+o(D)` and therefore sublinear.

Every slow-good interface satisfies

\[
N_i,L_i<C\sqrt D,
\qquad
B_i<{C\over\sqrt D},
\qquad
S_i+S_{i+1}<C\sqrt D.
\tag{GF.14}

The threshold grows, but remains `o(D)`.

---

## 2. Slow-good genuine TB2 interfaces are still sublinear

For a slow-good non-antipodal interface, the same common-column phase estimate gives

\[
\varepsilon_i
\le {C\sqrt D\over0.1567D-o(D)}
=O(D^{-1/2}).
\tag{GF.15}

A genuine minimal TB2 certificate therefore has both selected actual diameter directions within `O(D^-1/2)` of the projective normal to the row direction.  All row directions already lie in one shrinking projective window `omega_D=o(1)`.  Hence all actual diameter directions selected from slow-good TB2 certificates lie in one common projective interval of width

\[
\omega_D+O(D^{-1/2})=o(1).
\tag{GF.16}
\]

Switching supports at distinct interfaces are disjoint, so all selected certificate bars are distinct.  C67 separates distinct actual diameter directions by at least `1/D`.  Therefore an `o(1)` projective interval contains only `o(D)` such bars, and

\[
\boxed{|T_D^{\rm slow}|=o(D).}
\tag{GF.17}

---

## 3. A linear slow-good TB1 population automatically gives GF-A

Let `U_D^{slow}` be the slow-good non-antipodal TB1 interfaces, with one chosen TB1 bar per interface.

Suppose along a subsequence

\[
|U_D^{slow}|\ge\eta D
\tag{GF.18}
\]

for some fixed `eta>0`.  Choose

\[
\rho={\eta\over16}.
\tag{GF.19}
\]

For sufficiently large `D`, the shrinking row phase window has width below `rho`.  The projective `2rho`-ball around the common row phase has length `4rho`; C67 and switching-support disjointness show that at most

\[
4\rho D+O(1)
={\eta\over4}D+O(1)
\tag{GF.20}

chosen TB1 bars lie there.

Every remaining chosen TB1 bar is at least `rho` from both local dual-cone rays, exactly as in the strong-TB1 theorem.  Hence at least

\[
{3\eta\over4}D-o(D)
\tag{GF.21}

interfaces satisfy (GF.2).  This is GF-A.

Consequently, after passing to a subsequence, if GF-A does **not** occur then necessarily

\[
\boxed{|U_D^{slow}|=o(D).}
\tag{GF.22}

---

## 4. Without GF-A, all but `o(D)` shore interfaces are exactly antipodal

The slow-good interfaces partition into

\[
E_D^{slow}\sqcup U_D^{slow}\sqcup T_D^{slow},
\tag{GF.23}
\]

where `E_D^{slow}` is exact-antipodal.  Combine (GF.13), (GF.17), and (GF.22):

\[
|I_D\setminus E_D^{slow}|
\le |B_D|+|U_D^{slow}|+|T_D^{slow}|
=o(D).
\tag{GF.24}

Since `I_D` itself has positive linear length, this gives

\[
\boxed{
|E_D^{slow}|>{c\over2}D-o(D).
}
\tag{GF.25}

Thus exact antipodality occupies asymptotically all of the selected one-sided shore, except a sublinear separator set.

Let `R_D` be the number of maximal consecutive exact-antipodal runs.  Each pair of distinct runs requires at least one non-exact separator, so

\[
R_D\le |I_D\setminus E_D^{slow}|+1=o(D).
\tag{GF.26}

The longest run therefore has length at least

\[
{|E_D^{slow}|\over R_D},
\]

which tends to infinity because the numerator is `Omega(D)` while `R_D=o(D)`.  This proves (GF.4)--(GF.5).

---

## 5. A fixed linear population of wide exact force diamonds remains

Now fix the absolute angle

\[
\rho_0={c_0\over12}.
\]

The force-carrying robust fork gives

\[
|E_D^{force}(\rho_0)|+|S_D(\rho_0)|
>{c_0\over2}D-o(D),
\tag{GF.27}
\]

because `c_0-6rho_0=c_0/2`.

The strong family `S_D(rho_0)` is a subset of the non-antipodal TB1 interfaces.  In the no-GF-A branch those are sublinear by (GF.22), so

\[
\boxed{
|E_D^{force}(\rho_0)|>{c_0\over2}D-o(D).
}
\tag{GF.28}

For sufficiently large `D`, every original fixed-threshold good interface is also slow-good because `4C<C sqrt D`.  Hence all interfaces counted in (GF.28) belong to `E_D^{slow}` and are distributed among its `R_D=o(D)` exact-phase runs.

By pigeonhole, one exact-phase run contains at least

\[
W_D
\ge {|E_D^{force}(\rho_0)|\over R_D}
\tag{GF.29}
\]

wide exact force interfaces.  Equations (GF.26) and (GF.28) imply

\[
\boxed{W_D\to\infty.}
\]

Choose a run realizing this maximum.  This is the block in GF-B.

---

## 6. Force geometry inside the growing block

On the chosen run all coherent real row directions equal one vector `v_D`.  Write

\[
F_j=A_jv_D.
\tag{GF.30}
\]

The global one-sided switch functional gives

\[
\phi(F_j-F_{j+1})>0
\tag{GF.31}
\]

at every internal interface.  Therefore all scalar differences `A_j-A_{j+1}` have the same strict sign.  The sequence `A_j` is strictly monotone and all reactions point in one ordinary direction.

Telescoping gives

\[
\sum|G_j|
=\left|\sum G_j\right|
=|F_{p_D}-F_{q_D+1}|
=|A_{p_D}-A_{q_D+1}|<D,
\]

which is (GF.8).

At each of the `W_D` wide interfaces, the force-carrying robust theorem chooses two actual switching diameter bars satisfying

\[
G_j=D(\lambda_j^-d_j^-+\lambda_j^+d_j^+),
\qquad
\lambda_j^\pm>0,
\tag{GF.32}
\]

with projective gap at least `rho_0`.  The pair is vertex-disjoint and crossing for large `D`.  The central-diamond theorem then gives one common macroscopic central disk and one common `O(D^(3/4))` crossing zone for all these tagged pairs.

Thus the growing exact phase block contains a growing number of geometrically robust exact KKT dipoles, all tagged in order along one phase-constant shore and all carrying reactions in the same ordinary direction.

---

## 7. Frontier effect

This dichotomy removes a remaining form of fragmentation from the high-`t` branch.

```text
Either:
  non-antipodal TB1 obstructions survive at positive linear density
  with one fixed absolute interior-angle margin;

or:
  one consecutive exact-phase block has length -> infinity,
  its KKT resultant magnitudes are strictly monotone,
  total axial reaction variation is < D,
  and the same block contains W_D -> infinity
  fixed-angle exact-force central diameter diamonds.
```

The second branch is stronger than merely having linearly many exact-antipodal interfaces: the interfaces coalesce into a single growing exact phase plateau carrying a growing ordered force braid.

The block remains compatible with the known second-order exact-phase multishear cusp, so no deformation closure is claimed.  The next concrete target is to combine its exact interface order and common force axis with the common ordered near-triangular transverse train family.  Any countermodel must now realize a growing sequence of force-carrying central dipoles inside one exactly phase-constant train plateau.

No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** proved a subsequential dichotomy between a positive-density fixed-margin TB1 population and a growing exact-phase block containing a growing number of fixed-angle exact-force central diamonds with monotone collinear KKT reactions. C-II and Level I remain open.
